[FINAL-TEXT] === DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: csw1
diagnosis_text:
There is no physical link failure on the CSW1<->R1 segment. Both ends report a healthy Layer‑1/2 link: ethtool shows “Link detected: yes” on csw1 eth0 and r1 eth0, and L3 reachability on the point‑to‑point /30 (10.0.0.34 <-> 10.0.0.33) is intact (ICMP ping succeeds both directions from the specific interfaces).

The affected component is the OSPF process on CSW1, specifically the interface policy on eth0. After the change, CSW1’s FRR config has “interface eth0; ip ospf passive”. This suppresses OSPF Hellos on eth0, so the adjacency with R1 cannot form even though the link is up. Evidence: “show ip ospf neighbor” on csw1 lists peers on bond0/eth3/eth4/eth5/eth6 but not on eth0; on r1 there is only one OSPF neighbor via eth1 (to CSW2). Consequently, R1 lost the OSPF neighbor to CSW1 and all internal traffic prefers the CSW2 path, which monitoring misattributed to a physical link failure.

Localization: CSW1, interface eth0 (10.0.0.34/30) toward R1 (10.0.0.33/30). The root cause is a misconfiguration—OSPF made passive on CSW1 eth0 during the routine change.

Corrective fix: On CSW1, remove passive mode on eth0 in FRR:
- vtysh: configure terminal; interface eth0; no ip ospf passive; end; write memory.
This will restore OSPF Hellos and the CSW1<->R1 adjacency. No recabling is required at either end. 
=== END ===